{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>A woman zonah.</b> Rashi explained: one who had intercourse by an act of Israelite intercourse. One may ask: why is she disqualified by this for an ordinary priest? For we find no Tanna who says that an unmarried man who has intercourse with an unmarried woman thereby makes her a zonah, except for Rabbi Eliezer, and the halakhah is not like him. Rather, zonah means one who had intercourse, to disqualify, as Rashi himself explained in the chapter beginning Elu Na'arot." "<b>And a chalalah.</b> Who is not publicly known as a zonah. <b>And a chalalah</b> — the vav in place of 'or.' So too, 'and a woman divorced from her husband' — and likewise a divorcee and a chalalah." "<b>Divorced from her husband.</b> And not from a man who is not her husband. This is what Rabbi Elazar ben Matya derived: suppose her husband went overseas and they told her, 'Your husband has died,' and she became betrothed to another, and afterward her first husband came. From where do we know that she is permitted to return to the first one? For even though the latter gave her a get, he did not disqualify her from the priesthood. Scripture says, <b>divorced from her husband</b> — and not from another man."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The abbreviation חז״ק resolved as חזר וקשה ('one returns and asks'), a standard rabbinic query formula; the text as transmitted includes Rashi's name, which is rendered as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:14:05.270868103Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}